    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The County of Toulouse grew out of the Carolingian reorganisation of the south-west after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>’s campaigns in Aquitaine and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. In 778 Charlemagne appointed a Frankish noble, Chorson, as count of Toulouse; after Chorson was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basques">Basques</a> and deposed in 790, the county passed to William of Gellone, a kinsman of the king, who defended the Spanish March against Umayyad raids and took part in the capture of Barcelona in 801. William later retired to the monastery he had founded at Gellone, now Saint-Guilhem-le-Désert, and was venerated as a saint. Through the ninth century the county was contested among Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnates</a>, but from the 840s a family descended from Count Fredelo and his brother Raymond I established a hereditary hold over Toulouse and Rouergue. Historians call this line the Raymondines, after the name its counts used for generations. As royal authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-francia">West Francia</a> weakened, the counts gathered rights over Quercy, Albi, Nîmes and Narbonne, and in the tenth century Raymond III Pons appears in some documents with the title of duke of Aquitaine. By about 1100 the counts of Toulouse also claimed the titles of duke of Narbonne and marquis of Provence, which made them the most powerful lords between the Garonne and the Rhône.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mediterranean.jpg" alt="Mediterranean" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">of the modification : Eric Gaba (Sting), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The lands of the counts of Toulouse were among the most urbanised and prosperous in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>. Toulouse, on the Garonne, was a major market for the grain and wine of the surrounding plains, and the routes linking the Atlantic, by way of the Garonne, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ports of Narbonne, Montpellier and Saint-Gilles carried cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a>, salt and leather. Saint-Gilles, the family’s ancestral seat near the Rhône delta, was a busy port and a pilgrimage centre on the road to Santiago de Compostela, and its abbey church received a celebrated sculpted façade in the twelfth century. Toulouse itself was dominated by the great Romanesque basilica of Saint-Sernin, whose altar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> consecrated in 1096. Society was marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a> among the nobility, much land held as free allods rather than fiefs, and a strong urban patriciate. Southern towns had sizeable Jewish communities, and Occitan, the <em>langue d’oc</em>, was the language of daily life, of much legal writing and of literature. The courts of the counts and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> patronised the troubadours, among them Peire Vidal, a native of Toulouse. From the mid-twelfth century the dualist Cathar movement spread among artisans, merchants and lesser nobles of the region, organising its own bishoprics and finding protection among lords who tolerated it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#first-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-crusade.jpg" alt="First Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#first-crusade">First Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_the_Hermit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The counts of Toulouse never commanded a standing army; their forces were drawn from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a>, town militias and, increasingly, hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a> known as <em>routiers</em>. Their most celebrated campaign was abroad. Raymond IV, also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond of Saint-Gilles</a>, took the cross in 1095 and led probably the largest contingent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, accompanied by the papal legate Adhemar of Le Puy. He fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and from 1102 besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tripoli">Tripoli</a>, building the castle of Mont Pèlerin outside it; he died there in 1105, and the city fell in 1109 to his son Bertrand, founding the County of Tripoli. At home the counts fought a long struggle, sometimes called the Great Southern War, against the counts of Barcelona for Provence and the allegiance of the Languedoc nobility. They also faced the dukes of Aquitaine, who claimed Toulouse by marriage: William IX occupied the city twice, and in 1159 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> besieged it until Louis VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> entered Toulouse to support Raymond V and Henry withdrew. The heaviest blows came during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, when Simon de Montfort defeated Raymond VI and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a> at the Battle of Muret in 1213. Toulouse fought back: in 1218 Simon was killed by a stone from a siege engine while besieging the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#richard-i-of-england"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england.jpg" alt="Richard I of England" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_Fontevraud_Abbey_Richard_I_effigy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adam Bishop, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The counts ruled less through administration than through lordship, alliance and prestige. Their territory was a patchwork: Toulouse, the Toulousain and parts of the Albigeois and Quercy were held fairly directly, but powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> such as the Trencavel viscounts of Albi, Béziers and Carcassonne, the viscounts of Narbonne and the counts of Foix and Comminges shifted allegiance between Toulouse and Barcelona-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> as interest dictated. The counts were nominally vassals of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, but for long periods had little contact with them. Their power rested on castles, rights of justice, tolls and the revenues of towns, managed by local officers called vicars and bailiffs. Toulouse itself gained a large measure of self-government: by the mid-twelfth century a body of consuls, later known as capitouls, governed the city, and in the decades around 1200 the consulate extended its control over the surrounding countryside and dealt with the counts almost as an equal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diplomatic-marriage">Dynastic marriages</a> tied the house to the great royal families: Raymond V married Constance, sister of Louis VII of France, and Raymond VI married Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>, who brought the Agenais as her dowry. Written law and notarial culture, rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-law">Roman law</a>, were strong, as across the Midi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#paris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="Paris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Tour_Eiffel_vue_de_la_Tour_Saint-Jacques,_Paris_ao%C3%BBt_2014_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yann Caradec from Paris, France, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The county’s legacy lies above all in the culture of the Occitan south. Under the protection of the counts and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a>, troubadour lyric became the first great vernacular poetry of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, shaping the tradition of courtly love that passed to the trouvères of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, the Minnesänger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> and poets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberia</a>. The ruin of the southern courts during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> scattered the troubadours, yet Toulouse kept the tradition alive: in 1323 a group of its citizens founded the Consistori del Gai Saber, whose poetry contest, the Jeux floraux, still exists. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">crusade</a> itself left deep marks. It helped bring about the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, active in the county from the 1230s, and it shaped the Dominican order, whose founder Dominic of Caleruega preached among the Cathars and established his first house of preachers at Toulouse in 1215. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> of 1229 obliged the count to pay for teachers in Toulouse, and the university founded that year still exists. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> East the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tripoli">Tripoli</a>, created by Raymond IV’s crusade, survived until 1289. The Occitan epic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Albigensian Crusade</em> and the memory of the Cathars later became central to regional identity in Languedoc.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#albigensian-crusade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade.jpg" alt="Albigensian Crusade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_massacre_des_Albigeois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The county’s end came through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>. After the papal legate Peter of Castelnau was murdered in January 1208, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> blamed Raymond VI and proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">crusade</a> against the Cathars and their protectors. In 1209 a northern French army massacred the people of Béziers and took Carcassonne, and Simon de Montfort assumed its leadership. Raymond VI, excommunicated and outmanoeuvred, allied with his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a>, but their defeat at Muret in 1213 left Simon master of the south, and the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 granted him most of Raymond’s lands. Raymond VI and his son, the future Raymond VII, fought back, retaking Beaucaire in 1216 and Toulouse in 1217, and Simon’s death in 1218 seemed to reverse the conquest. The intervention of the French crown proved decisive: Louis VIII’s crusade of 1226 broke resistance, and by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> of 1229 Raymond VII kept a reduced county for life, while his only daughter Joan was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse of Poitiers</a>, brother of Louis IX. A last revolt in 1242, in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, failed. Raymond VII died in 1249, and when Alphonse and Joan died childless in 1271, the county was united with the royal domain of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The County of Toulouse was the greatest principality of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, a home of troubadour poetry and the launching point of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tripoli">Tripoli</a>; its destruction in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> brought Languedoc under the French crown and helped give rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> and the Dominican order.</em></p>

